<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Bataan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1942">1942</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Bataan was fought from January to April 1942 on the Bataan Peninsula of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luzon">Luzon</a>, where American and Filipino forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/douglas-macarthur">Douglas MacArthur</a> withdrew after the Japanese invasion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>. Short of food and medicine, about 75,000 defenders surrendered on 9 April 1942, and thousands died on the Bataan Death March to prison camps.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-bataan.jpg" alt="Battle of Bataan" width="320" height="210" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Philippine_Islands_Jap_tanks_full.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Under War Plan Orange 3, the American plan of April 1941, the Philippine garrison was to withdraw to the Bataan Peninsula, the key to Manila Bay, if the enemy landed in strength, and hold it to the &quot;last extremity&quot;. MacArthur rejected the plan as defeatist and won approval to defend the whole archipelago at the beaches, with supplies spread behind them. When the main force of Masaharu Homma's Fourteenth Army landed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lingayen-gulf">Lingayen Gulf</a> on 22 December 1941 and the beaches could not be held, the old plan was revived. By the end of the year Bataan held 15,000 Americans, 65,000 Filipinos and 26,000 refugees, but food for only about two months instead of the six the plans required.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>General Jonathan Wainwright's North Luzon Force fell back through five defensive lines to keep the road to Bataan open. From 9 January 1942 the Japanese attacked the main line at Abucay in the east and Mauban in the west. A roadblock behind Wainwright's flank led MacArthur to order a withdrawal to the Pilar-Bagac Road on 22 January. Japanese landings behind the lines on the west coast were destroyed in the Battle of the Points, and two battalions that broke through the new line were wiped out in the Battle of the Pockets. By 8 February Homma had only three effective infantry battalions and halted his attacks.</p><p>The defenders were by then on a starvation diet. MacArthur was ordered out and left Corregidor on the night of 12 March; Edward P. King took command of the Luzon Force. On 3 April a bombardment by 196 guns opened Homma's final offensive along Mount Samat, the 41st Division gave way, and the line collapsed before the surrender of 9 April.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The fall of Bataan hastened that of Corregidor a month later. The stand cheered the Allied peoples after the loss of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a> and the Indies. MacArthur's intelligence officer, Willoughby, later claimed it upset the Japanese timetable decisively, while the historian Gavin Long held that the Japanese had let resistance on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luzon">Luzon</a> run on in order to speed their conquest of the Indies. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>, 9 April is a national holiday, the Day of Valor, and the Shrine of Valor stands on Mount Samat.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Bataan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Japan invaded in December 1941, and after the fall of <b>Bataan</b> and Corregidor the government went into exile in Washington.</blockquote><a href="/state/commonwealth-of-the-philippines">Commonwealth of the Philippines</a></li></ul></section>
